\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Stock Indexes Slide Again; Sol Strengthens Slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leslie Josephs   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2008\par 23:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes slid again on Thursday, despite higher prices for most base and precious metals and gains on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results, the Lima Stock Exchange's general index fell 2.62% to end at 10,884.43.\par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index decreased 2.97% to finish at 17,940.91, while the mining subindex fell 3.20%. Mining stocks comprise more than half of the main index.\par \par \uc2 Zinc mining company \uc2 Volcan Compania Minera\uc2  SAA (VOLB.VL) lost 6.98% to end at 2.00 soles ($0.67), and tin mining company Minsur SAA dropped 5.74% to close at PEN5.75.\par \par \uc2 But precious metals company Compania Minera Buenaventura SAA (BVN) gained 9.51% to close at PEN64.50 amid rebounding gold prices.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Peru's finance minister and Central Reserve Bank president said that the country will be able to withstand impacts from the U.S. and global economic crises. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde pointed to high foreign reserves and strong economic growth. Peru's economy grew 9% last year and similar growth is forecast for this year.\par \par \uc2 But the statements did little to calm Peru's markets. Despite gains on Wall Street on Thursday, Peru's main indexes continued the steady slide that began on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "There's definitely a sense of pessimism and more aversion to risk," Gustavo Urrutia, an analyst at brokerage Centura SAB told Dow Jones Newswires. "People are looking for something safe, such as gold, like with Buenaventura."\par \par \uc2 It wasn't clear when Peru's indexes would recover, said Urrutia, who called the market trend "emotional."\par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday to sell $117 million at an average rate of 2.9751 Peruvian soles to the U.S. dollar. That followed Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that saw the sale of $42 million.\par \par \uc2 The sol ended slightly stronger at a midrate of PEN2.9745 to the dollar, compared with the previous session's close at PEN2.9755 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 -By Leslie Josephs, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-211-2689; peru@dowjones.com [ 18-09-08 2107GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080918e49i0010j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
