\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Stock Indexes End Lower; Sol Weakens A Bit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2008\par 22:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes ended lower Thursday as a number of blue chip shares declined.\par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results, the Lima Stock Exchange's general index fell 0.13% to end at 12,062.24.\par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index decreased 0.05% to finish at 20,123.75.\par \par \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN) declined 1.7% to end at 58.00 soles ($19.51) on weaker gold prices. The share price is now considerably lower than its 52-week high of $42.89.\par \par \uc2 Financial holding \uc2 Credicorp Ltd\uc2  (BAP) declined 1% to end at $73.03.\par \par \uc2 Zinc and lead miner \uc2 Volcan Compania Minera\uc2  SAA (VOLB.VL) lost 0.4% to end at PEN2.79 in heavy trade.\par \par \uc2 Southern Copper Corp\uc2 .(PCU) went the other way, rising 0.6% to end at $20.45 as copper prices increased. The share is sharply off from a 52-week high of $47.75.\par \par \uc2 The sol ended slightly weaker Thursday, at PEN2.9730 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The sol ended the previous session at PEN2.9710 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell a one-day record $363 million.\par \par \uc2 That follows \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell $323 million last week. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase $76 million Monday, $162 million Tuesday, and $99 million Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  follow a period of weakening of the sol, as investors continue to bet the U.S. dollar will strengthen.\par \par \uc2 A market analyst said the demand for the U.S. dollar was seen Thursday from both local and foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 In a report, Banco de Credito said there were indications the sol could start to strengthen again.\par \par \uc2 It pointed to the ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and to the possibility the Central Reserve Bank of Peru will increase its reference rate by 25 basis points to 6.50%.\par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-211 2652; peru@dowjones.com [ 09-11-08 1636ET ]\par \par \uc2 1071 1837 5077   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020080911e49b0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
