\par\par \b \uc2 Peru cenbank sells dollars to ease sol's retreat\b0\par\par\uc2 238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2008\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, June 23 (Reuters) - Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday for the first time in more than two years as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed, sending the Peruvian sol lower, traders said.\par \par \uc2 They said the bank had sold $14 million, but that was not enough to stop the sol <PEN=PE> closing weaker at 2.942/2.943 per dollar on Monday, down from Friday's close of 2.932/2.936 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marked an abrupt change from \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's usual policy of buying dollars to put the brakes on the sol, which has rallied as much as 15 percent against the dollar over the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pumped some $8.7 billion into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stop the sol firming too much in order to keep exports competitive.\par \par \uc2 Robust growth and investment-friendly policies led by President Alan Garcia have bolstered the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the government fears Peruvians borrowing heavily in dollars could suffer if the sol weakens.\par \par \uc2 Peru's economy, one of the fastest-growing in the world, expanded some 9 percent last year. ($1 = 2.9420 Peruvian soles <PEN=PE>) (Reporting by Ricardo Serra; Writing by Dana Ford; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-PERU/SOL|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080623e46n001qh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley1