\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Reuters Summit-Peru  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  may clamp down again   \b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters Latin America Investment Summit, click on   {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment08?pid=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment08?pid=500  }}}\uc2 ) (Adds quotes, background)   \par \par \uc2 By Terry Wade   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, March 28 (Reuters) - Peru may further tighten monetary policy if inflation speeds up, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde said on Friday, adding that the Andean economy could overheat despite a U.S. slowdown.   \par \par \uc2 Inflation spiked 0.91 percent in February, a four-year high that pushed inflation for the 12 months through February to 4.8 percent. Economists are waiting to see if inflation will ease in March, or get worse.   \par \par \uc2 "If inflationary pressures start to accelerate, we will take measures, and among those could be the interest rate or bank deposit requirements," said Velarde, who spoke in Lima at the Reuters Latin America Investment Summit. "We aren't ruling anything out."   \par \par \uc2 To curb inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised deposit requirements for banks in January and March, and in previous months raised its benchmark rate, now at 5.25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry has also helped out by slashing tariffs on food imports, lowering fuel taxes, and cutting spending to put a damper on inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Velarde has lately leaned in favor of raising deposit requirements, rather than interest rates. Higher rates could cause \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which in turn would create risks for borrowers and lenders if the Peruvian sol later depreciates. About half of all loans in \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 "When we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, it's in large part because we are worried about what could happen with the financial system," he said. "If the U.S. economy doesn't just slow down but enters a recession that drags down commodities prices, we worry our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could depreciate."   \par \par \uc2 GROWTH, OVERHEATING   \par \par \uc2 Velarde said the Andean economy, which grew 9 percent last year, could overheat if domestic demand continues to surge. Last year, domestic demand led growth, an unusual trend for a country that has long relied on exporting minerals.   \par \par \uc2 "There is a risk of overheating if internal demand keeps growing at a double-digit pace," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Velarde said he expects the economy to grow 7.5 percent in 2008, higher than a previous estimate of 7 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it expects economic growth of 9.5 percent in February, up from the same month a year ago.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target is 2 percent, plus or minus a 1 percentage-point tolerance band. Last year inflation was nearly 4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "Obviously, I'm concerned about inflationary pressure, but I don't think there's any chance we will lose control of it," he said. "I'm convinced that it could take a while to return to the (target) range but we will return to it."   \par \par \uc2 Most of the recent inflation has come from food imports, for products like wheat.   \par \par \uc2 "With monetary policy measures, I can't avoid the inflation attack that comes from abroad and the jump in food prices," Velarde said.   \par \par \uc2 He said a U.S. economic slowdown, trouble with credit markets in major financial centers, and high prices for agricultural commodities are raising doubts.   \par \par \uc2 "What's happening is that the world has never been so uncertain," he said. (For summit blog:   {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://summitnotebook.reuters.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://summitnotebook.reuters.com/  }}}\uc2 ) (Additional reporting by Teresa Cespedes, editing by Braden Reddall)   \par \par \uc2 LATAM-SUMMIT/VELARDE (UPDATE 1, PIX)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080403e43v001hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
